> **Update (June 2026):** This post was revised to align with the newer framework in [From irresponsible AI to evidence-based editorial guidance](/posts/20260609-iterative-pattern-framework/). The core extraction method here still stands; the June post adds maturity staging, measurement discipline, and trust-but-verify review gates.

> **tl;dr**: Export your best content from your CMS, use AI to extract quantitative patterns (word counts, title formats, tag usage), and deploy those patterns as guidance for an AI assistant. The same documentation works for human editors too.

The examples here use Drupal, but the approach works with any CMS — WordPress, a headless setup, or even Word documents.

Here's what we're aiming for — an AI assistant producing content aligned with patterns extracted from our best work:

> **User**:
>
> Summarize this publication: [uploads 24-page academic paper on disaster memorial parks in Japan]

> **Copilot response**:
>
> **Title**: Place Governance and Citizen-Driven Placemaking: Lessons from Disaster Memorial Parks after the 2011 Japan Tsunami (108 chars)
>
> **Body** (181 words): This publication examines the transformation of lost places through government-led planning and citizen-driven placemaking in disaster memorial parks following the 2011 Great East Japan Earthquake and Tsunami...
>
> **Themes**: Urban risk and planning, Recovery planning, Community-based DRR, Governance
>
> **Hazard**: Tsunami
>
> **Countries**: Japan, Asia

Or reviewing a draft:

> **User**:
>
> Here is my draft news update. What do you think? What tags should I use?
>
> [pastes 400-word draft about a new early warning system partnership]

> **Copilot response**:
>
> **Assessment**: Your draft is 412 words — slightly above our 350-word target for news updates. Consider trimming the background section.
>
> **Structure**: Good use of H2 subheadings (matches 69% of our high-performing news articles). The lead paragraph effectively answers who/what/when.
>
> **Suggested themes**:
> - Early warning (primary — directly relevant)
> - Governance (partnership announcement)
> - Science and technology (system implementation)
>
> **Suggested hazards**: Multi-hazard (the system covers multiple hazard types)
>
> **Style note**: "Aims to" appears twice — consider varying. Also, "utilize" → "use" per our plain language guidelines.

This can then be easily copy-pasted or used in an API to seed corrections or new content as drafts.

But simply uploading your PDF into ChatGPT will give you a response far from your editorial needs and CMS architecture.

Here's how to get there.

## Good guidelines in, good content out

"Keep it concise." "Use clear language." "Tag appropriately." Terse guidance spread over assorted documents in different systems. Editorial guidelines are rarely suited for humans and even worse for AI.

When guidelines are clear and in one place, they help humans — and they're what AI needs too.

The good news is we can get there by extracting rules from our existing good content.

Your best content contains what we need: patterns — word counts, title structures, tagging conventions. AI is exceptionally good at finding these patterns across hundreds of examples — something that would take humans weeks to analyze manually.

But how can we get that data to the LLM for analysis?

## Step 1: Extract the best examples

You don't want the latest 50 published items — you want the *best* 50. Automated queries give you recent content; curation gives you exemplary content.

We had an advantage: our Drupal instance already had `longevity` and `interestingness` fields that editors used to flag content quality. These aren't standard fields — they were added years ago for homepage curation — but they gave us a procedural way to isolate high-quality examples.

```php
<?php
// Custom Drush command (simplified)
public function exportExamples($content_type, $limit = 50) {
    $nids = \Drupal::entityQuery('node')
      ->condition('type', $content_type)
      ->condition('status', 1)
      ->condition('field_longevity', 'evergreen')  // Premier content
      ->condition('field_interestingness', ['high', 'featured'], 'IN')
      ->sort('created', 'DESC')
      ->range(0, $limit)
      ->execute();

    foreach ($nids as $nid) {
      $node = Node::load($nid);
      $output = "# " . $node->getTitle() . "\n\n";
      $output .= $node->get('body')->value . "\n\n";
      $output .= "Word count: " . str_word_count(strip_tags($node->get('body')->value)) . "\n";
      $output .= "Tags: " . implode(', ', $this->getTermNames($node, 'field_themes')) . "\n";
      file_put_contents("export/{$nid}.md", $output);
    }
}
```

If you don't have curation fields, you can filter by engagement metrics, editor picks, or manual review. The point: be intentional about what counts as "exemplary."

## Step 2: Analyze with AI

_Note: This is a somewhat simplified how-to, but you can enhance as needed._

For me the above yielded 122 examples exported to Markdown. I asked an LLM (in my case Claude Code) to extract quantitative patterns and generate agent guidance:

```
You are designing system-level agent guidance for a domain-specific AI assistant.

You are provided with a file named EXAMPLE_CONTENTS.md containing approximately 100 high-quality Markdown examples that represent "correct" and production-ready output for our website.

Your task is to ANALYSE EXAMPLE_CONTENTS.md and INFER:
- Structural patterns and recurring sections
- Length and formatting norms
- Title and summary conventions
- Metadata usage and controlled vocabularies
- Implicit rules (what always appears, what is optional, what never appears)

... truncated, see full version in foldout below
```

